    bio: 'Epictetus was born a slave, likely in Hierapolis in Phrygia (in present-day Turkey), and spent his early life in Rome as the property of Epaphroditus, a freedman and secretary to the Emperor Nero. According to ancient sources, his master once broke his leg to demonstrate that Epictetus was indifferent to pain — to which Epictetus reportedly replied, calmly, that he had warned him it would break. He studied Stoic philosophy under Musonius Rufus while still enslaved, was eventually freed, and began teaching philosophy in Rome until the Emperor Domitian banished philosophers from the city around 89 CE.\n\nAfter his expulsion from Rome, Epictetus established a school in Nicopolis, in northwestern Greece, where he taught for the remainder of his life. He wrote nothing himself; what survives of his philosophy comes from the careful notes taken by his student Arrian, who compiled them into the Discourses and the shorter Enchiridion (or Handbook). These texts reveal a philosophy of extraordinary psychological precision and practical urgency — a Stoicism stripped of its more speculative metaphysical commitments and focused entirely on the question of how to live. His school attracted students from across the Roman world, and Marcus Aurelius, who never met him, was profoundly shaped by his writings.\n\nEpictetus lived simply, reportedly keeping almost no possessions, and embodied in his own life the philosophy he taught. The central distinction of his thought — between what is "up to us" (our judgments, desires, and will) and what is "not up to us" (our bodies, reputations, property, and external events) — was not an abstract thesis but a discipline he had tested against the most extreme conditions a human being can face. His influence on later Stoicism, on early Christian asceticism, on Renaissance humanism, and on modern cognitive therapy has been immense, though his name is less celebrated than it deserves to be.',
